1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,143
Welcome to Prompt Engineering Fundamentals.

2
00:00:02,143 --> 00:00:03,857
I'm glad you're here.

3
00:00:03,857 --> 00:00:06,000
Over the next thirty minutes,

4
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:10,714
you'll learn how to talk to AI models in a way

5
00:00:10,714 --> 00:00:13,714
that actually gets you what you want.

6
00:00:13,714 --> 00:00:18,429
We'll cover five core principles, eight practical techniques, four advanced patterns,

7
00:00:18,429 --> 00:00:21,000
and the most common mistakes —

8
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:24,429
so you don't have to make them yourself.

9
00:00:24,429 --> 00:00:29,143
If you've ever asked ChatGPT, or Claude, or Gemini something —

10
00:00:29,143 --> 00:00:32,571
and gotten back an answer that was fine,

11
00:00:32,571 --> 00:00:36,857
but not quite right — this course is for you.

12
00:00:36,857 --> 00:00:38,571
The interesting thing is,

13
00:00:38,571 --> 00:00:43,286
the same model that gave you that mediocre answer is capable

14
00:00:43,286 --> 00:00:45,857
of giving you something genuinely useful.

15
00:00:45,857 --> 00:00:47,571
The model didn't change.

16
00:00:47,571 --> 00:00:48,857
The prompt did.

17
00:00:48,857 --> 00:00:51,429
So let's start with a definition.

18
00:00:51,429 --> 00:00:56,143
Prompt engineering is the craft of writing inputs — prompts —

19
00:00:56,143 --> 00:01:00,857
that guide AI language models to produce the output you actually

20
00:01:00,857 --> 00:01:02,057
need.

21
00:01:02,057 --> 00:01:06,343
I want you to think of it less as coding,

22
00:01:06,343 --> 00:01:09,771
and more as learning to brief somebody well.

23
00:01:09,771 --> 00:01:14,486
If you've ever managed a contractor, or onboarded a new hire,

24
00:01:14,486 --> 00:01:16,629
you already know this skill.

25
00:01:16,629 --> 00:01:20,057
The clearer your brief — the more context,

26
00:01:20,057 --> 00:01:22,629
the more specific the deliverable —

27
00:01:22,629 --> 00:01:25,629
the better the work that comes back.

28
00:01:25,629 --> 00:01:27,343
Same idea, different audience.

29
00:01:27,343 --> 00:01:29,057
Why does it matter?

30
00:01:29,057 --> 00:01:30,343
Three things, mainly.

31
00:01:30,343 --> 00:01:33,771
First — a well-written prompt saves you time.

32
00:01:33,771 --> 00:01:36,343
You stop going back and forth,

33
00:01:36,343 --> 00:01:38,914
asking the AI to redo something,

34
00:01:38,914 --> 00:01:42,343
because you got it right the first time.

35
00:01:42,343 --> 00:01:46,629
Second — it produces results that actually fit your context.

36
00:01:46,629 --> 00:01:47,829
Your audience.

37
00:01:47,829 --> 00:01:49,029
Your tone.

38
00:01:49,029 --> 00:01:50,229
Your constraints.

39
00:01:50,229 --> 00:01:54,943
The prompt is the part where your specific situation gets translated

40
00:01:54,943 --> 00:01:57,943
into something the model can act on.

41
00:01:57,943 --> 00:01:59,229
And third —

42
00:01:59,229 --> 00:02:03,086
and this is the one most people miss —

43
00:02:03,086 --> 00:02:06,086
it unlocks capabilities the model already has,

44
00:02:06,086 --> 00:02:10,800
but that you'd never see if you ask it the wrong

45
00:02:10,800 --> 00:02:12,000
way.

46
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:16,714
The model knows more than your average prompt is asking it

47
00:02:16,714 --> 00:02:17,914
to do.

48
00:02:17,914 --> 00:02:21,343
Let me show you what this looks like.

49
00:02:21,343 --> 00:02:26,057
Imagine I ask the AI to "write about dogs." That's it.

50
00:02:26,057 --> 00:02:27,257
Three words.

51
00:02:27,257 --> 00:02:30,686
What I'll get back is technically about dogs.

52
00:02:30,686 --> 00:02:32,400
But it'll be generic.

53
00:02:32,400 --> 00:02:33,600
Broad.

54
00:02:33,600 --> 00:02:37,886
Mostly useless if I had a real reason for asking.

55
00:02:37,886 --> 00:02:42,600
Now compare that to: "Write a 200-word article explaining why dogs

56
00:02:42,600 --> 00:02:46,029
make excellent therapy animals for children with autism.

57
00:02:46,029 --> 00:02:50,314
Include specific behavioral traits, and cite recent research." Same model.

58
00:02:50,314 --> 00:02:51,600
Same response time.

59
00:02:51,600 --> 00:02:52,886
Wildly different output.

60
00:02:52,886 --> 00:02:57,600
The second prompt got me a focused piece of writing I

61
00:02:57,600 --> 00:03:02,314
could actually publish — or at least edit lightly and publish.

62
00:03:02,314 --> 00:03:03,600
What's the difference?

63
00:03:03,600 --> 00:03:04,886
Three things, really.

64
00:03:04,886 --> 00:03:07,457
Clarity — about what I want.

65
00:03:07,457 --> 00:03:10,029
Specificity — the details that matter.

66
00:03:10,029 --> 00:03:13,457
And structure — the way I framed it.

67
00:03:13,457 --> 00:03:17,314
Those three ideas underpin everything else in this course.

68
00:03:17,314 --> 00:03:21,600
Here's what to expect from the rest of the videos.

69
00:03:21,600 --> 00:03:26,314
Sessions two and three give you the foundation — five principles,

70
00:03:26,314 --> 00:03:28,886
then seven of the eight techniques.

71
00:03:28,886 --> 00:03:31,029
Session four covers refinement —

72
00:03:31,029 --> 00:03:35,743
the eighth technique plus four advanced patterns you'll use for production

73
00:03:35,743 --> 00:03:36,943
work.

74
00:03:36,943 --> 00:03:39,943
Session five covers the mistakes everyone makes,

75
00:03:39,943 --> 00:03:42,514
plus three habits for safer prompting.

76
00:03:42,514 --> 00:03:46,800
And session six wraps up with the quiz, the workbook,

77
00:03:46,800 --> 00:03:49,371
and what to take with you.

78
00:03:49,371 --> 00:03:54,086
Quick note before we dive in: you don't need to know

79
00:03:54,086 --> 00:03:57,086
how to code to follow this course.

80
00:03:57,086 --> 00:04:01,800
Every technique we'll cover works in any chat-based AI tool —

81
00:04:01,800 --> 00:04:03,943
ChatGPT, Claude, Gemini, the works.

82
00:04:03,943 --> 00:04:06,514
There is an optional code track,

83
00:04:06,514 --> 00:04:10,800
if you want to try them through an API —

84
00:04:10,800 --> 00:04:12,514
but it's just that.

85
00:04:12,514 --> 00:04:13,714
Optional.

86
00:04:13,714 --> 00:04:17,143
The real skill lives in the prompt itself,

87
00:04:17,143 --> 00:04:21,857
and the prompt is the same whether you type it into

88
00:04:21,857 --> 00:04:25,714
a chat box or send it through a script.

89
00:04:25,714 --> 00:04:29,143
The companion workbook has four hands-on practice tasks.

90
00:04:29,143 --> 00:04:33,857
You'll get the most out of this course if you treat

91
00:04:33,857 --> 00:04:38,571
the videos as the what, and the workbook as the how.

92
00:04:38,571 --> 00:04:40,714
Pause where you need to,

93
00:04:40,714 --> 00:04:45,000
run your own examples in your AI tool of choice,

94
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:49,286
and build up a prompt library you'll actually use later.

95
00:04:49,286 --> 00:04:50,486
Alright.

96
00:04:50,486 --> 00:04:52,200
In the next session,

97
00:04:52,200 --> 00:04:56,914
we'll cover the five core principles that make any prompt better.

98
00:04:56,914 --> 00:04:58,200
Let's go. ---
